++so for a bare `- path/to/file` bullet (no backticks, no ` — reason`) it already
++returned `path/to/file` unchanged. The new `else:` branch produces the identical
++result, so this test does not exercise any behavior the old code got wrong and
++violates the review rule that each new test must fail against pre-change code.
++
++Fix: either remove `test_parser_bare_path_no_separator`, or change it to cover a
++case the old parser actually mishandled — the discriminating gap is a **bare path
++WITH a description separator** (e.g. `- path/to/file — reason` or
++`- path/to/file - reason`), which the old code returned as `path/to/file — reason`
++(wrong) and the new `else:`/separator-split returns as `path/to/file` (right).
++Keep the adversarial over-exemption tests (multiple-backtick-spans-yields-first,
++empty-backtick-span-skipped) — those are correctly discriminating and are the
++security-critical coverage; do not weaken them.
